# knob-default-spellings.manifest — the CHECKED presence of each spelling,
# your-org/nexus-code#995.
#
# Generated by:  bash monitor/watcher/test-knob-default-agrees.sh --emit-spellings
# Enforced by:   bash monitor/watcher/test-knob-default-agrees.sh
#
# <VAR><TAB><mask over A B C E>, `-` where that spelling is ABSENT:
#   A  the config-key default in _config.sh — what PRODUCTION runs. Present for
#      every row by construction: it is what defines the population.
#   B  _config.sh's own malformed-value fallback (`[[ … ]] || VAR=N`).
#   C  a consumer's `${VAR:-N}` — unreachable in production, taken by tests.
#   E  the human-readable `(default N)` in a docstring.
# D (a consumer function's local-variable fallback) is deliberately NOT in the
# mask: it exists only where the suite's hand table names a file/function/local,
# so pinning it would pin the hand table rather than the tree.
#
# WHY A PRESENCE MASK AND NOT THE VALUES. The guard asserts AGREEMENT, never a
# specific number — pinning values would add a sixth place to forget. What it
# cannot get from the tree alone is whether an ABSENT spelling is absent BY
# DESIGN or absent because a rename made an extraction silently return "". That
# distinction is the whole of #995's "the model does not extend": absence must
# be legal, and it must not be free. This file is where it stops being free.
#
# REGENERATING THIS FILE IS NOT A FIX. A changed row means a knob gained or
# lost a spelling of its default. Decide which, check the remaining spellings
# still agree, and say so in the commit message.
#
# Measured at f40a538: 100 knobs. Only 6 carry all four spellings; 20 carry A
# alone. That distribution is the argument against "just add 65 more rows to
# the table" — most knobs legitimately have nothing to compare.
